# =============================================================================
# main — `main exit 3 { … }` runs its body and then ends the process with exit code 3, with no `@ cli`.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • `main exit N` names the process exit code the program ends with: the
#     body runs normally and its output is kept
#   • The exit code is part of the entry, so no `@ cli` annotation or
#     command-line surface is needed
#   • A plain `main` ends with exit code 0
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Expecting `exit 3` to stop the body early: it names the code the
#     program ends with after the body ran
#   • Expecting a non-zero code to be an error report: it is only the
#     status the process returns
#
# See also: incipit, salve-munde, exitus
# =============================================================================

main exit 3 {
    print "start"
    mut int n ← 1
    n ← n + 1
    print n
    print "done"
}
